= Version control — Technical =

== Revision model ==
A version-control system represents a set of revisions and relationships among them.

Depending on the system, revisions may be modeled as:
* deltas;
* snapshots;
* directed acyclic graphs of commits;
* content-addressed objects;
* centralized sequential revisions.

The logical history model and physical storage representation should be distinguished.

== Branching and merging ==
A branch represents a line/reference of development. Merge operations attempt to combine changes from divergent histories.

A merge conflict is not necessarily a semantic conflict; textual changes can merge cleanly while producing an incorrect program, and textual overlap can conflict even when the intended semantic combination is obvious.

== Distributed version control ==
Distributed systems such as Git allow repositories to contain substantial/full history locally and exchange objects/references among peers.

"Distributed" describes repository architecture, not project governance. Projects can still designate canonical remotes, protected branches, review requirements, or release authorities.

== Content addressing ==
Git identifies objects by hashes over object content/metadata representation. This supports integrity checking and deduplication-like behaviors, but a version-control hash should not automatically be interpreted as a complete provenance or trust system.

== Delta systems ==
RCS stores reverse deltas for text revisions to conserve space and reconstruct earlier versions. Other systems use different delta directions, snapshots, compression, or hybrid pack formats.

The historical evolution from SCCS and RCS to networked and distributed systems reflects changing collaboration and storage models.

== Configuration management ==
Version control typically manages revisions of selected configuration items. Full software configuration management may additionally govern:
* baselines;
* builds;
* releases;
* dependencies;
* environment state;
* approvals/change control;
* status accounting.

== Provenance limits ==
Commit metadata provides valuable lineage, but can be rewritten, imported, squashed, mirrored, or disconnected from external build/runtime inputs. Strong reproducibility may require artifact hashes, dependency locks, signed attestations, environment capture, and external provenance records.

